  "body": "Found in the M5 review of PR #2112 (#2031, rest Adaptive on proved remaining stock).\n\nWhat is missing:\n- **Entry proof, raster half unpinned.** Removing the raster half of the entry proof, or setting its footprint to 0, leaves all 9 `rest_adaptive` tests green: the polygon proof alone carries every fixture. Nothing fails if the raster half breaks.\n- **Load ceiling only on the planner's own grid.** The tests hold engagement to optimal x 1.05 (15.3 % of D) measured on the planner's D/120 grid. On the default D/80 measuring grid, `a_conventional_cut_leaving_stock_on_the_walls_keeps_every_law` reads 16.7 % of D (the author saw up to 17.4 %). This looks like the measuring rim over-reading wall on a coarse grid, not real over-engagement, but no test pins a ceiling at the default grid.\n\nDone means:\n- A fixture where only the raster half of the entry proof refuses an entry (it goes red when that half is removed or the footprint is 0).\n- A corner assertion on a fine grid (D/240, on a fixture small enough for the 4096-cell cap) and a documented load ceiling on the default D/80 grid (about 1.2 x optimal), both pinned.\n- Filed before #2033 wires rest Adaptive into the Pocket operation.\n\nRefs #2031, PR #2112.\n",
